WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.291
Hi, I'm Odessa!

00:00:02.291 --> 00:00:06.296
Keeping a race car's pose locked at two hundred fifty kilometres an

00:00:06.296 --> 00:00:08.633
hour means rethinking where the work happens.

00:00:10.475 --> 00:00:14.354
Move the heavy geometry onto the GPU, keep a small filter on

00:00:14.354 --> 00:00:18.881
the processor, and one parameter set holds from a city street to a racetrack.

00:00:21.008 --> 00:00:22.145
A laser scan arrives.

00:00:22.145 --> 00:00:25.555
The map is a flat hash table on the GPU, so thousands

00:00:25.555 --> 00:00:28.681
of threads search it at once with no tree to walk.

00:00:29.101 --> 00:00:32.784
A sparsity-aware matcher aligns the scan to the map, and keeps the

00:00:32.784 --> 00:00:35.547
sparse far points a strict version would throw away.

00:00:35.967 --> 00:00:39.713
The aligned pose drops to a small Kalman filter that fuses it

00:00:39.713 --> 00:00:42.522
with the inertial sensor a hundred times a second.

00:00:42.942 --> 00:00:46.461
It projects the slightly old pose forward to now, streams a smooth

00:00:46.461 --> 00:00:48.514
result, and the scan joins the map.

00:00:49.858 --> 00:00:53.493
The registered pose is already a few milliseconds old when it lands.

00:00:53.913 --> 00:00:57.298
So the filter adds how much its own estimate moved in between,

00:00:57.298 --> 00:00:59.837
and corrects to the present instead of the past.

00:01:01.092 --> 00:01:04.789
On the hardest racetrack the error drops to about fifteen and a

00:01:04.789 --> 00:01:07.561
half metres, well under every baseline that even finished.

00:01:08.967 --> 00:01:12.706
And it does it in about nineteen milliseconds a scan, far below

00:01:12.706 --> 00:01:15.510
the delay where the racing estimate starts to diverge.

00:01:17.350 --> 00:01:22.042
FAR-LIO moves the geometry onto the GPU and leaves a tiny delay-compensating

00:01:22.042 --> 00:01:25.951
filter on the CPU — one config, street to racetrack.

00:01:27.233 --> 00:01:33.213
To recap: a GPU voxel map, a sparsity-aware matcher, and a tiny delay-compensating filter.

00:01:33.213 --> 00:01:37.057
One parameter set, two hundred fifty kilometres an hour.

00:01:37.477 --> 00:01:39.656
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:39.656 --> 00:01:40.279
I'm Odessa.

00:01:40.279 --> 00:01:40.590
Bye!
